\section{Appendix structure}
\label{sec:rh_appendix}
% 2026-10-26 (integration, JFEc): the roadmap rewritten for the restructured online appendix (header of this file);
% the previous roadmap and note S13 are parked below. Items for files the section agents have not written yet print
% only once the file exists. Bullets only (author, 2026-10-26).
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Data and predictors} (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_data}): the construction of the data and the predictors,
  and the predictor dictionary (Table~\ref{tab:rh_dm_dictionary}).
\item \textbf{Implementation details} (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_impl}): samples and windows, the training scheme in
  practice, the tuned hyperparameters and the tuning procedures.
% 2026-10-26 (coordinator): unwrapped \IfFileExists{rehaul/A_methods.tex} (the file exists; the flat Overleaf build has no rehaul/ directory, so the wrapper skipped its body there).
\item \textbf{Estimators, implementation and tuning}
  (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_methods}): the estimators in full, the selection splits, and how the frozen
  hyperparameters were obtained, with checks of them.
% 2026-10-26 (coordinator): unwrapped \IfFileExists{rehaul/A_results.tex} (the file exists; the flat Overleaf build has no rehaul/ directory, so the wrapper skipped its body there).
% 2026-10-28 (v2 of Sections 6-7, author approved preliminarily): the two items below now list their subsections,
% among them the new FOMC statement days and ordinary days (app:rh_channels) and the every-bar feature-count ladder
% (app:rh_fi_ladder), both cited from Sections 6 and 7. Was: "\item \textbf{Additional results on forecast accuracy}
% (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_results})." and "\item \textbf{Variable importance: details} (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_sec_fi})."
\item \textbf{Additional results on forecast accuracy}
  (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_results}): samples and spans, subperiods, calibration and the squared-error loss, the design
  of the predictor-group ablations, FOMC statement days and ordinary days, with the rule fixed before the day types
  were scored (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_channels}), the tests beyond HAR\,+\,RM\,+\,FOMC, and the closing-interval HAR
  benchmark.
% 2026-10-26 (coordinator): unwrapped \IfFileExists{rehaul/A_fi.tex} (the file exists; the flat Overleaf build has no rehaul/ directory, so the wrapper skipped its body there).
\item \textbf{Variable importance: details} (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_sec_fi}): the construction and checks of the
  attribution, the attribution shares and rankings, the conditional shares, the response shapes and interactions, the
  window test, the feature-count ladder on every bar (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_fi_ladder}) and the grid of training
  windows and numbers of series.
\item \textbf{Economic application: details} (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_econ}): the straddle's sample, quotes and
  execution; the Sharpe ratio and its alternatives; position sizing, compounding and the interval-shift test;
  where the profits come from; market exposure.
\item \textbf{Robustness} (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_robust_stat}): the estimation window, which was chosen after
  out-of-sample scoring and is reported at every window scored; the training sample (all \rhdmNIntervals{}
  half-hour intervals, the closing half hour alone, equal numbers of training rows); and the sessions the futures
  trade on NYSE holidays.
\item \textbf{Statistical tests} (Appendix~\ref{sec:rh_methods_tests}): the Diebold--Mariano test, the Newey--West
  variance, the fixed-$b$ test of a mean return, the bootstrap tests of equal Sharpe ratios, and the model
  confidence set, the superior predictive ability test and StepM.
\item \textbf{Closing-interval models} (Appendix~\ref{app:rh_closing}): the closing-interval HAR benchmark, rebuilt
  under the current data chain, and the pooled models re-estimated on the closing half hour alone; the accuracy of
  the closing half hour on both evaluation spans; the multiple-comparison procedures.
\end{itemize}

% 2026-10-26 (integration, JFEc; plan: "Reproducibility appendix (9 pp) becomes a replication-package README with a
% half-page pointer"): the section Reproducibility (app:rh_repro) is cut to this pointer; its text (the generator of
% each number, the software list, the unrecorded hashes) is parked at the end of this file and belongs in the
% package README. Most of its nine printed pages were the floats of 10_appendix_floats.tex, which now print in the
% sections that cite them.
\subsection{Replication package}
\label{app:rh_repro}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Contents.} The code that builds the design matrices from the data, fits every model and writes every
  forecast, and the generators that turn the stored forecasts into the numbers, tables and figures of the paper. Its
  README maps each number, table and figure to its generator and inputs.
\item \textbf{Numbers.} The numbers in the text are macros written by these generators
  (\texttt{writeup/\allowbreak make\_*.py}) to files in \texttt{writeup/\allowbreak generated/}; one switch
  (\texttt{REHAUL\_\allowbreak FINAL\_\allowbreak RUN}, \texttt{writeup/\allowbreak final\_run.py}) points them at the
  final forecasts.
\item \textbf{Fits.} The linear models are fitted by
% 2026-10-28 (author: prune "pooled"). Was: "The pooled linear models are fitted by".
  \texttt{experiments/\allowbreak pooled\_\allowbreak linear\_\allowbreak rerun\_\allowbreak 2026\_10\_19\_13.py} and
  the tree ensembles by \texttt{experiments/\allowbreak pooled\_\allowbreak trees\_\allowbreak rerun\_13.py}, the
  random forest with Intel's oneDAL engine
  (Intel Extension for Scikit-learn).
\item \textbf{Software.} \texttt{requirements.txt} names the packages, with a version range for each; it is not a
  pinned lock file. The package does not record hashes of the design matrices, the CPU class of the fits or their
  compute time.
\end{itemize}

